<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Asia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> is the eastern region of Asia, usually taken to include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. For two thousand years its societies shared Chinese characters, Confucian ideas of government and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a>, and were linked by a tribute system centred on the Chinese court. Today it is one of the most populous and economically important regions of the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-asia.jpg" alt="East Asia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Koyos + Ssolbergj + Serg!o · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Asia_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> is a geocultural region of Asia. It borders North Asia to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> to the south-west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to the west, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> to the east. In common usage it covers Greater China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. China, Japan and Korea are its three core countries and civilisations, since they once shared a written language, a culture and a Confucian system of values instituted by imperial China. Some scholars include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, part of the wider Chinese cultural sphere, though many organisations class it as Southeast Asian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chinese civilisation is regarded as one of the earliest cradles of civilisation, and for thousands of years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was the leading civilisation of the region. Han China's northeastern expansion in 108 BCE took the northern part of the Korean peninsula, and Chinese writing, coinage, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> culture and Confucian political institutions took root in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Japan adopted Chinese characters from the fourth century. Chinese influence was strongest under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, and in 645 Prince Naka no Ōe launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taika-reform">Taika Reform</a>, remaking the Japanese bureaucracy on the Tang model. From the 3rd to the 18th century a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary system</a> governed relations: the Chinese emperor received tribute and in return conferred legitimacy on other rulers and gave gifts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s power declined as contacts with the West grew in the late 19th century. Commodore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Matthew C. Perry</a> opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> to Western influence, and after the Meiji Restoration Japan became the first industrialised nation in East Asia. It defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in the First Sino-Japanese War and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1905. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, Japan brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, much of eastern China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> under its control. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> was proclaimed on 1 October 1949, and the Republic of China government withdrew to Taiwan. The Korean peninsula was partitioned, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> lasted from 1950 to 1953.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The major languages are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandarin-chinese">Mandarin Chinese</a>, Japanese and Korean, and the major religions include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> and Chinese folk religion, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The region has around 1.65 billion inhabitants. The Chinese calendar is the root of many other East Asian calendars, and East Asian vocabularies and scripts are often derived from Classical Chinese.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “East Asia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jin dynasty changed the political geography of <b>East Asia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ivory and gold were carried down the Limpopo valley to coastal ports, first Chibuene and later Sofala, and in return came great quantities of glass beads from India and South-<b>East Asia</b>, as well as Chinese celadon, which has been found at the royal residence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mapungubwe">Kingdom of Mapungubwe · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ming Empire had great influence in <b>East Asia</b> and beyond during the early modern period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ryukyu Kingdom turned a small chain of islands into one of <b>East Asia</b>'s leading maritime trading states, and its unusual double subordination to Ming and Qing China and to Japan's Satsuma Domain made it a meeting point of the Chinese and Japanese worlds until Meiji Japan annexed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Divine Bell of King Seongdeok, completed in 771, is one of the largest surviving bells in Korea, and the Cheomseongdae tower at Gyeongju, traditionally dated to Queen Seondeok's reign, is often described as the oldest surviving observatory in <b>East Asia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A founding member of ASEAN in 1967, Singapore hosts the secretariats of APEC and the Pacific Economic Cooperation Council, belongs to the Commonwealth, the Non-Aligned Movement and the <b>East Asia</b> Summit and keeps diplomatic relations with more than 180 states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
